                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma_3, \mathbf{B}G_{conn}] \stackrel{[\Sigma_3, \mathbf{c}_{conn}]}{\to} [\Sigma_3, \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)_{conn}] \stackrel{\exp(2 \pi i \int_{\Sigma_3}(-))}{\to} U(1)
          %p
            The first line we have already discussed (
            %a{:href => "#FiorenzaSchreiberStasheff"}> FSS
            ). The second line is implicit in (
            %a{:href => "#CJMSW"}> CJMSW, def. 3.3, prop. 3.4
            ). There it is shown that there is a natural
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \times
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+ordinary+differential+cohomology"} fiber integration in ordinary differential cohomology
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+circle+3-bundle"} Chern-Simons circle 3-bundle with connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi con
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{con}
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi can
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla_{can} : G \times S^1 \to \mathbf{B}G_{conn}
          %p
            such that its
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunct"} adjunct
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi conn
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bar \nabla_{conn} : G \to [S^1 , \mathbf{B}G_{conn}]
          %p
            has the property that postcomposition with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi c
                    %mi conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(2 \pi i \int_{S^1}[S^1, \mathbf{c}_{conn}])
            modulates the WZW 2-bundle. This is precisely the content of the second line in the table above.
          %h3#PerturbativeQuantization Perturbative quantization
          %p
            Some pointers regarded the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of Chern-Simons theory:
          %h4#FeynmanPerturbationSeries Feynman perturbation series
          %p
            The standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            based on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+propagator"} Chern-Simons propagator
            (see there for more) was discussed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AxelrodSinger"} Axelrod-Singer 91, 93
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes+on+compactified+configuration+spaces+of+points"} Feynman amplitudes on compactified configuration spaces of points
          %p
            This led
            %a{:href => "#Kontsevich"} Kontsevich 94
            to suggest that the perturbative Chern-Simons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            serves to exhibit a
            = succeed "-model" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+complex"} graph complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fulton-MacPherson+compactification"} Fulton-MacPherson compactification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+spaces+of+points"} configuration spaces of points
            and for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+knots"} spaces of knots
          %p
            For the first case this was proven in
            %a{:href => "#LambrechtsVolic14"}> Lambrechts-Volic 14
            \.
          %p
            For the second case see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "graph+complex#CattaneoCottaRamusinoLongoni02"} CattaneoCottaRamusinoLongoni02
            %a{:href => "graph+complex#Volic13"} Volić 13
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BarNatan91"} Bar-Natan 91
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robbert+Dijkgraaf"}> Robbert Dijkgraaf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Perturbative topological field theory
              %em Trieste 1993, Proceedings, String theory, gauge theory and quantum gravity ‘93
              189-227 (
              %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/399223/"}> spire:399223
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/DijkgraafPerturbativeCS93.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %h4#PerturbativePathIntegralQuantization Path integral quantization
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Witten89"} Witten (1989), section 2
            indicates the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
            of Chern-Simons theory and finds that the result after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            by a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is the sum over equivalence classes of classical solutions, i.e. of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connections"} flat connections
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+systems"} local systems
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mi fl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{fl}
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the exponentiated Chern-Simons action functional
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi CS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i k \, S_{CS}(\nabla))
                of the classical solution;
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+torsion"} analytic torsion
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi g
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mi fl
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(g,\nabla_{fl})
            %li
              %p
                the exponentiated
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta+invariant"} eta invariant
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi fl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\pi i\, \eta(\nabla_{fl}))
                (
                %a{:href => "eta+invariant#OnOddDimensionalManifolds"} hence
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Selberg+zeta+function"} Selberg zeta function
                twisted by the given
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+system"} local system
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mi fl
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{fl}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi k
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi CS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo →
                      %mn ∞
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi fl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi i
                    %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi CS
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi fl
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi i
                    %mi π
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi fl
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mi T
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mi fl
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{}{\int} \exp(i k \, S_{CS}(\nabla)) (D\nabla)
                  \\;\stackrel{k \to  \infty}{\longrightarrow}\;
                  \\underset{[\nabla_{fl}]}{\sum}
                  \\exp\left(i k S_{CS}\left(\nabla_{fl}\right)\right)
                  \\exp\left(i \pi \eta\left(\nabla_{fl}\right) \right)
                  T\left(g,\nabla_{fl}\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Witten89"}> Witten 89 (2.17)
            )
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "eta+invariant#OnManifoldsWithBoundary"} eta invariant – Boundaries, determinant line bundles and perturbative Chern-Simons
          %p
            Now this expression is not independent of the chosen metric
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Levi-Civita+connection"} Levi-Civita connection
            of the metric (
            %a{:href => "#Witten89"}> Witten 89 (2.23)
            ) (and is then well-defined after choosing an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah+2-framing"} Atiyah 2-framing
          %p
            Notice that here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i k
            is
            = succeed ",so" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tfrac{i}{\hbar}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \to \infty
            is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+limit"} semiclassical limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
          %h4#PerurbativeBVDeformationQuantization BV deformation quantization
          %p
            We discuss the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            of Chern-Simons theory to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra+of+local+observables"} factorization algebra of local observables
            along the lines of
            %em
              %a{:href => "renormalization#OfTheoriesInBVForm"} renormalization – Of theories in BV-CS forms
            (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello
            ).
          %p
            We first discuss the classical and quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+complex"} BV-BRST complex
            of the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TheBVBRSTComplexOfTheUnderlyingFreeFieldTheory"} The BV-BRST complex of the underlying free field theory
          %p
            and then perturbatively introduce the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalizing
            and solving the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#TheRenormalizedQuantumMasterEquation"} The renormalized quantum master equation
              \.
          %h5#TheBVBRSTComplexOfTheUnderlyingFreeFieldTheory The BV-BRST complex of the underlying free field theory
          %p
            Fix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathfrak{g}, \langle -,-\rangle_{\mathfrak{g}})
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            equipped with a binary and non-degenerate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            (for instance a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebra"} semisimple Lie algebra
            with
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            we consider only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            between the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            we regard the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction+term"} interaction term
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∝
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \mapsto I(A) \propto \int_\Sigma \langle A \wedge [A \wedge A]\rangle
          %p
            as a perturbation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action+functional"} kinetic action functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi kin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∝
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \mapsto S_{kin}(A) \propto \int_\Sigma \langle A \wedge d A\rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            of the full theory is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Lie
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CE(Lie(G\mathbf{Conn}(X)//C^\infty(X,G)))
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lie
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lie(\Omega^1_\Sigma(-,\mathfrak{g})//[X,G]
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+differentiation"} Lie differentiation
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+group"} smooth group
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            acting on the fields, which are the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+valued+1-forms"} Lie algebra valued 1-forms
          %p
            So the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            of Chern-Simons theory has
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                the 1-forms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^1(\Sigma, \mathfrak{g})
            %li
              %p
                and as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghosts"} ghosts
                the
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^0(\Sigma, \mathfrak{g})
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+complex"} BV-BRST complex
            hence has in addition
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antifields"} antifields
                the local duals of the fields, which under the evident
                = succeed "-and-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+product"} wedge product
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integration
                pairing is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^2(\Sigma, \mathfrak{g})
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antighosts"} antighosts
                similarly
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^3(\Sigma, \mathfrak{g})
          %p
            In summary the fields, ghosts, antifields and antighists form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham complex
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensored
            with
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            (with the notation as dicussed there) Chern-Simons theory on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            has the sheaf of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{E}
                  \=
                  \\Omega^\bullet_\Sigma(-, \mathfrak{g})
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here we should regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            as being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded
            and homogeneously of degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            (this is the natural grading on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            regarded as an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"} L-infinity algebra
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet_\Sigma(-,\mathfrak{g})
            is already the correct BV-BRST grading
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"}> field
                  \:
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghost+fields"} ghost fields
                %th
                  genuine
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antifields"} antifields
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antighost+fields"} antighost fields
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ∈
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_\Sigma^0(-,\mathfrak{g})
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_\Sigma^1(-,\mathfrak{g})
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_\Sigma^2(-,\mathfrak{g})
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_\Sigma^3(-,\mathfrak{g})
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} degree
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} -1
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 0
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 2
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antibracket"} antibracket
            is given by the canonical local pairing obtained by taking the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+product"} wedge product
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} \otimes \mathfrak{g}
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle-,-\rangle_{\mathfrak{g}}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integration of differential forms
            over
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1, \phi_2 \in \Omega^\bullet(-,\mathfrak{g})
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{\phi_1, \phi_2\}
                  \=
                  \\int_\Sigma \langle \phi_1 \wedge \phi_2\rangle_{\mathfrak{g}}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            we take the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functions"} linear functions
            on these fields, hence the (graded-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+algebra"} symmetric algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sym
                  %mover
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym \overline{\mathcal{E}}
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathcal{E}}
            %a{:href => "elliptic+chain+complex#AtiyahBottLemma"} Atiyah-Bott lemma
            we may in the present situation take the observables equivalently to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compactly supported
            sections of the dual field bundle, with duality induced by the local pairing
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Dens
                      %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}_c \otimes \mathcal{E}_c \to Dens_\Sigma
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f \colon \Omega^0(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g}) \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            is then presented by an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar f \in \Omega^3(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g})
            via the evaluation map:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f \;\colon\; \phi \mapsto \int_\Sigma \bar f \wedge \phi
                  \\,.
          %p With this identification we have in summary the following situation:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  linear local
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghost+field"} ghost field
                  observables
                %th
                  genuine
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
                  observables
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antifield"} antifield
                  observables
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antighost+field"} antighost field
                  observables
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ∈
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi cp
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_{cp}^3(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g})
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi cp
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^2_{cp}(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g})
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi cp
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_{cp}^1(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g})
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi cp
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_{cp}^0(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g})
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} degree
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 0
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} -1
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} -2
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
            on the sections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            induces a differential on the observables by dualization.
          %p
            Hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+BV-complex"} classical BV-complex
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Obs
                    %mi free
                    %mi cl
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cp
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi dR
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Obs^{cl}_{free}
                  \=
                  \\left(
                  Sym (\Omega^\bullet_{cp}(\Sigma, \mathfrak{g})), Q = (d_{dR})^\ast, \langle -,-\rangle^\ast = \int_\Sigma (-) \wedge (-)
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Equipped with the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-Laplacian"} BV-Laplacian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            discussed at
            %em
              %a{:href => "free+field+theory#TheQuantumObservables"} free field theory - The quantum observables
            this yields the corrsponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+BV-complex"} quantum BV-complex
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Obs
                    %mi free
                    %mi q
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi Sym
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cp
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Q
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi dR
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Obs^{q}_{free}
                  \=
                  \\left(
                  Sym (\Omega_{cp}^\bullet(\Sigma, \mathfrak{g})[ [\hbar] ], Q = (d_{dR})^\ast + \hbar \Delta, \langle -,-\rangle^\ast = \int_\Sigma (-) \wedge (-)
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %h5#TheRenormalizedQuantumMasterEquation The renormalized quantum master equation
          %p
            Next we want to add to the above free field theory the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            term
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo +
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q + \hbar \Delta
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Obs
                    %mi free
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Obs^q_{free}
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo +
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q + \{I,-\} + \hbar \Delta
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            in order to be well defined.
          %p
            This is discussed for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Costello"}> Costello, section 15
            ).
          %p (…)
          %h2#quantum_chernsimons_theory Quantum Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#RTConstructionAndEquivalenceToGeometricQuantization The Reshetikhin-Turaev construction and its equivalence to geometric quantization
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+model"} Reshetikhin-Turaev model
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+TQFT"} 3d TQFT
            had traditionally been expected to be quantized Chern-Simons theory. A proof if this requires showing that the RT-constuction is equivalent to the result of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GeometricQuantization"} above
          %p
            The proof of this is not entirely spelled out in the literature, but all the ingredients seem to be known, involving results such as (
            %a{:href => "#Andersen12"}> Andersen 12
            ).
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            See also the MathOverflow-discussion
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/86792/why-hasnt-anyone-proved-that-the-two-standard-approaches-to-quantizing-chern-si"} Why hasn’t anyone proved that the two standard approaches to quantizing Chern-Simons theory are equivalent?
          %h3#ShiftedLevel Shifted/renormalized level
          %p
            Upon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+%28Chern-Simons+theory%29"} Chern-Simons level
            receives a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+Coxeter+number"} dual Coxeter number
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            %a{:href => "Chern-Simons+level#LevelRenormalization"} there
            for more.
          %h3#observables_knot_polynomials Observables: knot polynomials
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+line"} Wilson line
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "#Witten89"} Witten (1989)
            it was shown that the new
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} polynomial invariant
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knot
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vaughan+Jones"} Vaughan Jones
            in the context of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
            – can be given a heuristic geometric interpretation: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
            V(q) of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            in a 3-manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            can be viewed as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            over all
            = succeed "-connections" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            of the exponential of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+form"} Chern–Simons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S[A]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi all
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi connections
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi on
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi M
                  %msub
                    %mi hol
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi iS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V_K(q) = \int_{all\,connections\,A\,on\,M} hol_A(K) \,\, exp(iS[A])
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi π
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi M
                  %mi Tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi dA
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 2
                    %mn 3
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S[A] = \frac{k}{4\pi} \int_M Tr (A \wedge dA + \frac{2}{3} A \wedge A \wedge A)
          %p
            is the integral of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+form"} Chern–Simons Lagrangian
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tr
                  %msub
                    %mi Hol
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Tr Hol_A (K)
          %p
            is the trace of the holonomy of the connection around the knot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+representation"} fundamental representation
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  q = e^{\frac{2 \pi i}{k+2}}.
          %p
            Said heuristically: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
            of the knot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            can be understood as the “average value” over all connections of the
            %em trace of the holonomy of the connection
            around the knot
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            to some other Lie group; the representation in which the trace is evaluated can also be altered. Each of these modifications gives rise to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
          %h3#as_an_extended_tqft As an extended TQFT
          %p
            The beautiful thing about Chern–Simons theory is that Witten was able use the
            %em locality
            property of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            to give a
            %em nonperturbative
            way to actually compute it. In this way Chern–Simons theory has become the ‘poster-child’ of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+quantum+field+theory"} extended topological quantum field theory
            since it exemplifies the main idea: take advantage of the higher gluing laws in order to compute geometric quantities.
          %p
            One of the major mathematical projects around Chern–Simons theory has therefore been to try and understand it rigorously as a 3-2-1-0
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+quantum+field+theory"} extended topological quantum field theory
          %p
            For the abelian case the major paper in this regard is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"} Topological Quantum Field Theories from Compact Lie Groups
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"} Freed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"} Hopkins
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Lurie
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Teleman"} Teleman
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            of dimension 0,1,2 and 3 are heuristically expected to be:
          %p
            Proposals by other authors include (
            %a{:href => "#Henriques15"}> Henriques 15
            ).
          %p
            A closed 3-manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ↦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
            the path integral given above (a number).
          %p
            A closed 2-manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ↦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
            the space of sections of the line bundle over the moduli space of flat
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            (a finite-dimensional
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
          %p
            A circle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ↦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+energy+representation"} positive energy representation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+group"} loop group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_k (G)
            at level
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            (a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+category"} linear category
          .query
            %p
              The R-T construction sticks on the circle the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
              of representations of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+group"} quantum group
              at a root of unity, modulo “unphysical representations.” Are these supposed to be the same? Is this just the Kazhdan-Lusztig equivalence?
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              \: the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev construction
              works with any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
          %p
            The 2-category assigned to the point is the most interesting piece of data since in principle all the other invariants can be derived from it using the gluing law. In the paper
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"} Topological Quantum Field Theories from Compact Lie Groups
          %p
            A point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ↦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
            the category of skyscraper sheaves on —, thought of as a 2-category via —.
          .query
            %p Bruce: I’ve run out of time here and I can’t precisely fill in those blanks above. Any help?
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ben+Webster"}> Ben Webster
              \: My understanding is that nobody is quite sure how to fill in those blanks. One line of thinking is that it should be an object in a 3-category with is
              %strong not
              2Cat.
          %p Other groups have conceptualized this differently (but most likely equivalently at the end of the day as)
          %p
            A point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ↦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mapsto
            the 2-category of unitary
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-representation"} 2-representation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Still others think of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            assigned to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            in different terms.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#tunneling_between_vacua__instanton_floer_homology Tunneling between vacua – instanton Floer homology
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            a fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            on the 3-dimensional base space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gradient+flow"} gradient flow
            of the Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi CS
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{CS} : \Omega^1(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g}) \to \mathbb{R}
            with respect to the respective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+inner+product"} Hodge inner product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^1(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g})
            characterizes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instanton"} Yang-Mills instanton
            solutions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \times \mathbb{R}
            with metric
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \otimes I
          %p
            This phenomenon is captured by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+Floer+homology"} instanton Floer homology
          %h3#as_an_effective_background_for_topological_string_theory As an effective background for topological string theory
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Witten94"}> Witten94
            ) an argument was given that Chern-Simons theory can be understood as the effective target space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            %a{:href => "TCFT#ActionFunctionals"} TCFT – Effective background theories
            for more on this.
          %h3#as_3dimensional_gravity As 3-dimensional gravity
          %p
            The Chern-Simons action functional for the case that the gauge group is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(2,1)
            (and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            is taken to be the one that pairs a translation generator with a rotation generator) happens to be equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            in 3-dimensions.
          %p
            Moreover, Chern-Simons theory for the groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(2,2)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(3,1)
            is similarly equivalent to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            in 3-dimensions.
          %p
            Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of Chern-Simons theory is fairly well understood, these identifications imply indeed a definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            in 3-dimensions.
          %p
            More on this is at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+gravity"} Chern-Simons gravity
          %p
            Beware that there is a subtlety in the definition of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Iso
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(2,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            then the configuration space naturally contains degenerate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
            fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            (notably the 0 vielbein) and hence the induced rank-2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = \langle E \otimes E\rangle
            may also be degenerate. Such degenerate tensors are not technically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            tensors, since these are required to be non-degenerate. The genuine non-degenerate metric tensors correspond to precisely those
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Iso
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(2,1)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Iso
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (O(2,1) \hookrightarrow Iso(2,1))
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
          %p
            However, the quantum theory exists nicely if one allows the larger configuration space of possibly degenerate metrics exists nicely, while the constrained one does not. This may be interpreted as saying that at least for purposes of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            it is wrong require non-degenerate metric tensors.
          %h3#holographic_relation_to_2d_wesszuminowitten_model Holographic relation to 2d Wess-Zumino-Witten model
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} AdS3-CFT2 and CS-WZW correspondence
          %h3#chernsimons_theory_and_modular_forms Chern–Simons theory and modular forms
          %p
            Trying to interest your
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory"} number theory
            friends with Chern–Simons theory? How about this: the Chern–Simons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z(k)
            above is (in a certain precise sense) a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+form"} modular form
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+form"} modular form
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Bartlett"} me
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                mock theta function
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/mock+theta+function"} ?
          %ul
            %li
              %p Lawrence and Zagier, Modular forms and quantum invariants of 3-manifolds, Asian Journal of Mathematics vol 3 no 1 (1999).
            %li
              %p
                Hikami, Quantum invariant, modular forms, and lattice points,
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0409016"} arXiv
          %h3#the_morse_theory_of_chernsimons_theory The Morse theory of Chern–Simons theory
          %p
            In a recent
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.columbia.edu/~woit/wordpress/?p=2357"} talk
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.columbia.edu/~woit/wordpress/?p=2357"} Not Even Wrong
          %blockquote
            %p
              The main new idea that Witten was using was that the contributions of different critical points p (including complex ones), could be calculated by choosing appropriate contours
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_p
              using
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse+theory"} Morse theory
              for the Chern–Simons functional. This sort of Morse theory involving holomorphic Morse functions gets used in mathematics in
              = succeed "." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Picard-Lefshetz theory
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Picard-Lefshetz+theory"} ?
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands"} geometric Langlands
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_p
              with integral coefficients and these coefficients jump at “Stokes curves” as one varies the parameter in one’s integral (in this case,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=k/n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              are large). In his talk, Witten showed the answer that he gets for the case of the figure-eight knot.
          %p
            For slides of Witten’s talk, click
            %a{:href => "http://www.princeton.edu/~tklose/adscft/EdwardWittenPCTS.pdf"} here
            and for video, click
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://echo360.princeton.edu:8080/ess/echo/presentation/2730cd4e-39b0-4d97-a825-165e7d4b819f"} here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            dimensions:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mi n
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi λ
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  I(\lambda) = \int_{\mathbb{R}^n} d^n exp(i\lambda f(x_1, \ldots, x_n)).
          %p
            We want to make sense of the integral when the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is allowed to take on imaginary values (naively, the integral diverges). To do this, we use Morse theory: we choose as our Morse function the real part of the exponent, that is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mi λ
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h = \Re(i \lambda f)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_p
            is an
            = succeed "-cycle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≪
                        %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_n (X, X_{\ll0})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            appearing in the integral over
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi p
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi p
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C = \sum_p n_p C_p
          %p
            In this way we can make sense of the integral
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            by
            %em defining
            it as the integral over these new cycles (“contours”):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mtext crit. points
                      %mi p
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi p
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  I(\lambda)  = \sum_{\text{crit. points} p} n_p I_p(\lambda)
          %p
            This new definition actually converges, and makes sense. Apparantly the same technique can be used to interpret the Chern–Simons path integral in the case of complex
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %h3#TraceAsAPathIntegral Traces as a path integral?
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Witten89"}> Witten 89
            ) is the observation that the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"}> trace
            ” occuring in the “trace of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            around the knot” term in the path integral should
            %em itself
            be seen as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/brucebartlett/published/Geometric+quantization+and+the+path+integral+in+Chern-Simons+theory"} Geometric quantization and the path integral in Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            For technical details on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
          %h3#HigherDimensionalGeneralization Higher dimensional generalizations
          %p
            One question that’s been bugging me (Ben Webster) recently is what fills in the analogy “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
            is to Chern–Simons theory as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Khovanov+homology"} Khovanov homology
            is to ??”
          %p
            (Urs: Answer now at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Khovanov+homology"} Khovanov homology
          %p
            Which is to say
            %em What 3/4-dimensional structure is Khovanov homology hinting at?
            I’m inclined to think there must be one, as it seems that all of the knot homologies associated by Chern–Simons theory to representations have categorifications (I have a
            %a{:href => "http://math.mit.edu/~bwebster/KI-HRT.pdf"} mostly finished paper
            on this). Presumably these all glue together into something, possibly by a similar trick to the Reshetikhin-Turaev construction of 3-manifold invariants, but it’s not so easy for me to see how.
          %h3#realization_in_physical_systems Realization in physical systems
          %p
            Chern-Simons theory has mostly been studied as a test case example for (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"}> pre
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            in theoretical physics and mathematics. Also in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            it appears in various incarnations and governs the hypothetical physics of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic
            relation to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            and the higher dimensional generalizations of this.
          %p
            But there are also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+systems"} physical systems
            that one can set up in a laboratory experiment which are described by at least some aspects of Chern-Simons theory at least in some limit. The most prominent such example is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+Hall+effect"} quantum Hall effect
            system in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            constrained to an effectively 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            and subject to perpendicular
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computing"} topological quantum computing
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
